  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA short wave trough is influencing the area with moisture \nconvergence and ascent to help promote thunderstorm and heavy \nshower activity which is along the higher elevations currently.\nHeavy downpours are due to PWATs close to 1.5 to 1.75 inches \nand a moisture flux off of Lake Michigan. Vorticity advection \nwill be prevalent this late afternoon across the northern and \ncentral areas of West Virginia as well as southeast Ohio and \nnortheast Kentucky. \n\nA lull should be in effect this afternoon with increasing \ncoverage expected as a shortwave passes through ahead of a cold\nfront draped across the Ohio valley which is forecast to move \nslightly closer to our area by this evening. This will be during\nthe max heating time frame and into the evening allowing for \nmore convection to ignite.\n\nAnother kink in heights for the late morning is forecast to \nbring in some energy and more moisture along with vorticity \nadvection which will cause some morning convection potential. \nSouthwest warm aid advection in the boundary layer will\ndestabilize the air mass along with upper level northwesterly \nflow which will likely advect in a weak MCS into the late \nmorning according to a few Hi-res models and the GFS. However, \nthe rest of Sunday looks to be quiet as weak high pressure tries\nto influence the area along with more of a zonal flow.\n\nMonday is under a risk of severe weather with additional \nmoisture as PWATs rise above the 1.5 inch threshold once again \nalong with the support of above environmental lapse rates. Weak\nsteering flow around 15 to 20kts will mean that backbuilding or\nslow moving storms could produce 1 to 2 inch rain rates per \nhour which could exceed flash flood guidance and promote \nlocalized flooding, especially in low lying or flood prone \nareas. \n\nWPC does have us under a marginal threat for excessive rainfall\nfor Monday, but wouldn't be surprised if a slight was \nintroduced in the near future. Quick up and down diurnal \nconvection could pose downbursts which may also cause isolated \nflash flooding or damaging wind which will be the main threats \ninto Tuesday.\n\nTuesday is under the influence of another short wave trough \nwith a kink in heights which will promote unsettled weather \nthrough Wednesday along with that influence of the \naforementioned cold frontal boundary lingering and meandering \nto our north. A slight risk for excessive rainfall has been \nplaced by WPC for Tuesday and Wednesday which will promote the \nmain threat of potential localized flooding issues. \n\nA cold front is then expected to arrive on Thursday with \nfrontal passage forecast by medium range models by Friday \nmorning. The weekend may bring some reprieve to unsettled \nweather as high pressure and more of a zonal flow regime builds \nin.",
